---
name: crustify-translator-test-recipes
description: Working recipes for sanitizer, Miri and coverage runs of the ffmpeg crate in a translator worktree, plus pitfalls hit in practice
metadata:
  type: reference
---
Shared by the orchestrator, translators and reviewers of [[ffmpeg-wrap-campaign-20260930]]; the
orchestrator curates it between waves (merged duplicates and removed stale notes on 2026-10-09).
## Running things
- Workspace root for cargo is `crustify/rust`; a background `cargo` started from the worktree root fails with "could not find Cargo.toml". `cd <dir> && (a) & (b) &` changes directory for `a` only: put the `cd` inside each backgrounded subshell. `mkdir -p logs && (a) & (b) &` likewise only sequences `a`: create log dirs in their own command first.
- Keep `CARGO_TARGET_DIR` outside the worktree (e.g. `/tmp/<batch>/{asan,tsan,bsan,miri}`): an untracked `target/` inside it is easy to wipe or commit.
- Never `pkill -f <pattern>`: sibling agents share the host and match too, and from the Bash tool the pattern is in the tool's own shell command line, so it kills that shell (exit 144). Find PIDs with `ps -eo pid,args | awk ...` or `pgrep -f`, then `kill <pid>`.
- Background jobs have died with exit 144 (a long `cargo bsan test`, a long wavefront `--update` loop): keep suites small enough to finish and rerun only the binaries still missing.
- `cargo test --workspace` interleaves C log output with harness lines (opt/cpu tests), so `awk` over `test result:` lines can miscount a phantom failure; judge by `grep -cE "FAILED|panicked"` and the per-binary `N passed; 0 failed` lines.
- Run `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings`: test files count too (a deliberate `Clone::clone` on a `Copy` type needs a local `#[allow(clippy::clone_on_copy)]`).
- `RUSTDOCFLAGS=-D warnings cargo doc -p ffmpeg` failed on `avstring.rs`'s ambiguous `[`format`]` link until the core wave-0 batch-12 review fixed it; check your own homes' doc warnings.
- The crate is not rustfmt-clean, and `rustfmt --edition 2024 <file>` can reformat unrelated hunks of a shared file (a `git show HEAD:<file> | rustfmt --check` pre-check did not predict it). After formatting, count `git diff -U0 <base> -- <file>` hunks; if one is not yours, rebuild the file as the base text plus your own block. A mechanical fix (formats wave-2 batch 1): copy the base file to `/tmp/x/base.rs` and `basefmt.rs`, `rustfmt` the latter, `diff -u base.rs basefmt.rs > U.patch`, then after formatting your file run `patch -R <file> < U.patch` — it reverts exactly rustfmt's unrelated hunks.
- `patch -R` leaves `<file>.orig` beside the file when a hunk applies at an offset: delete it before `git add`.
- The test files are not rustfmt-clean either. A `/tmp` copy of one that has `#[path = "../common/..."] mod` makes rustfmt fail on the missing path, and with `2>/dev/null` the pre-check then reports 0 unformatted hunks when there are some. Copy `tests/common/` next to the copy's parent, or keep stderr visible (formats wave-3 batch 2).
- To format only your own code in such a file: rustfmt a copy, then apply only the `difflib` opcodes (current -> formatted) that touch lines you added (base -> current); the leftover hunk count then equals the base's own (formats wave-2 batch 0). `rustfmt --check` on stdin exits 0 even for unformatted input. Re-run clippy after: rustfmt can wrap `assert_eq!(unsafe {..}, 0)` so the `// SAFETY:` line no longer sits above the `unsafe` (bind `let ret = unsafe {..};` first).
## Sanitizers
- ASan/UBSan, TSan: `CRUSTIFY_FFMPEG_VARIANT=<asan-ubsan|tsan> RUSTFLAGS=-Zsanitizer=<address|thread> CARGO_TARGET_DIR=<outside>/<unique> cargo +nightly test -Zbuild-std --target x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu --lib --tests -- --skip compile_fail`. Skip `compile_fail`: under `-Zbuild-std` rustc adds `$RUST/core` source notes, so trybuild `.stderr` blessed on stable mismatches. Whole-crate ASan and TSan passes take ~5 min each with a fresh target dir.
- C UBSan reports in the asan-ubsan build recover and print `runtime error:` without failing the test: grep the log for it.
- Tests that need `'static` heap strings (exactly sized, so ASan sees a read past the NUL) via `Box::leak` get reported as leaks by LSan (the lib binary exits 1 and, without `--no-fail-fast`, cargo skips the remaining binaries) and by Miri. Push each leaked `&'static CStr` into a `static KEEP: Mutex<Vec<&'static CStr>>`: memory reachable from a static is not a leak to either (formats wave-1 batch 2, `tests/ub_safe/pixdesc.rs::leaked`).
- C's whole-descriptor readers (`av_read_image_line2`, `av_write_image_line2`, `av_get_*bits_per_pixel`) trust the descriptor: mixing one format's flags with another's components (e.g. `BITSTREAM` on a byte format) makes C shift by a negative amount (`pixdesc.c:72`/`:152` UBSan, recovered). Equivalence sequences must keep every intermediate descriptor consistent; `PixelFormatDescriptor`'s setters do not range-check, so the wave-2 wrappers (`pixdesc::read_image_line`/`write_image_line`, `bits_per_pixel`, `imgutils::max_pixel_steps`) mirror C's int arithmetic first (`pixdesc.rs::line_plan`) and refuse undefined cases.
- Random setter sequences leave `ChannelLayout` counts anywhere up to `i32::MAX` (`set_unspecified(arg as i32)`). A later `retype(CUSTOM)` then `av_calloc`s and walks a map of up to `av_max_alloc` bytes (89M channels; 14 s plain). Counts above `INT_MAX / 24` are cheap, because C fails with ENOMEM up front. So cap only the 1024..=INT_MAX/24 range for map-allocating steps (formats wave-3 batch 2, `ub_safe/channel_layout.rs::order_changes`).
- LeakSanitizer flags a `HeapVec<T>` of owning elements (`Vec`, `String`): the `Heap` policy never drops elements. Use plain elements in tests that build or resize one.
- Header inlines in the `-sys` shim (`av_clip_*_c`, `av_bswap*`, `av_int2float`) are outside every sanitized and coverage C build. For UBSan evidence, rebuild the shim with trapping UBSan in its own target dir: `CC=clang CFLAGS="-fsanitize=undefined -fsanitize-trap=undefined"` (UB = SIGILL; traps on `av_clip_intp2_c(x, 32)`). Do not add `-fsanitize=unsigned-shift-base`: it traps on `av_bswap32`'s defined unsigned shifts.
- Validate a C function's UB model before writing wrapper guards: a 30-line C harness built with `clang -O1 -g -fsanitize=address,undefined -I<wt> -I<wt>/crustify/builds/asan-ubsan ... -L<wt>/crustify/builds/asan-ubsan/libavutil -lavutil -Wl,-rpath,...` and an xorshift loop over edge values finds `runtime error:` inputs in seconds (`av_q2intfloat`'s `sign<<31`, `av_rescale_delta`'s `2*a - b`; core wave-1 batch 2).
- To check whether a wrapper's guards over-reject (return `None` on input C handles): enumerate the refused inputs, then replay each in its own process with `UBSAN_OPTIONS=halt_on_error=1` and classify by exit status. UBSan reports each source location once per process, so correlating markers with `runtime error:` lines inside one process marks later refused inputs as clean when they are not (core wave-1 batch-1 review found `Rational::nearest` over-rejecting this way; a C division by zero also kills the process, so skip known-fatal candidates).
- OOM branches the `av_max_alloc` limit never reaches (e.g. `av_fast_realloc`, `fast_malloc`): an isolated-child test spawned through `sh -c 'ulimit -v 524288 && exec "$0" "$@"' <exe> --exact <name>` asking for 1 GiB fails them deterministically on plain and coverage builds. Skip it under asan-ubsan/tsan/bsan, whose runtimes cannot start under such a limit.
- A 2 GiB `vec![0u8; n]` passed to one C call is fine under plain/ASan/TSan (~1.4 s, ~1.2 GB RSS); skip it under BSan. Such sizes are needed for C `int` overflows (`av_memcpy_backptr`'s `blocklen <<= 1` at 2^30).
- TSan false positive: concurrent local-time `av_parse_time` calls (`parseutils::parse_date` without `Z`, or a missing date) race in glibc `tzset_internal` (free/strdup of the cached TZ, reached from `mktime`/`localtime_r`), which glibc serializes with its internal `tzset_lock` that TSan cannot see. Serialize the suite's local-time parses with a Rust `Mutex` (`LOCAL_TIME` in `equiv/` and `ub_safe/parseutils.rs`); UTC dates (`...Z`) never call `mktime` (options wave-0 batch 4).
## BorrowSanitizer (runtime `de6ede0149`, clang 23.1.3, installed 2026-10-08)
The workspace-wide BSan gate runs only in a sub-campaign's last review wave; otherwise BSan runs only on individual reproducers or BSan-prone tests ([[reviewer-bsan-policy]]). Check the known artifact classes below before investigating a report.
- `BSAN_OPTIONS=gc_hang_threshold_ms=600000 CRUSTIFY_FFMPEG_VARIANT=bsan cargo bsan test --lib --tests -- --skip compile_fail`, under `timeout`. Without the threshold, multi-threaded suites abort after 10 s with "garbage collection has been waiting ... for threads to reach a safepoint".
- It is slow: whole-crate equiv ~12.5 min and ub_safe ~25 min at the core wave-1 tip (growing each wave); the `log::vlog` equiv tests alone take ~8 min.
- Cost is per reference retag and grows with one long-lived allocation's history: filling a ~3 KiB buffer byte by byte took 45-130 s per fill and 34 GB RSS. Write multi-KiB buffers with one slice op (`copy_from_slice`, `ptr::write_bytes`) and view a growing array at checkpoints, not after every append.
- Never walk a buffer element by element through a slice iterator either: every `&u8` that `iter()`/`flatten()` yields is a retag. `writes.iter().flatten().flatten().any(|&b| b != 0)` over 16 planes of 1.5 KiB cost 26-47 s per call when the planes were all-zero (the walk reaches the end) and a `(0..1536).map(xorshift).collect()` image ~0.85 s; compare whole slices (`p[..] != ZERO[..]`, memcmp) and build test data as a `const` table + `to_vec()`. Found by per-format `Instant` timers in `equiv/pixdesc.rs` (formats wave-1 batch-0 review: 1432 s -> 215 s, 215 s -> 7 s). The remaining cost there is real volume: ~30 ms per C observation (`av_read_image_line2` + `av_write_image_line2` x4 with 16 fresh 1.5 KiB `Vec`s, whose 16 frees alone take ~0.36 s).
- `[T]::fill` on a `Copy` non-`u8` element (`[MaybeUninit<u8>]::fill(MaybeUninit::new(0))`), `iter_mut().zip(..)` writes and `iter().all(|&b| ..)` compares are all one retag per byte, in library code as much as in tests: `Image::alloc`'s zero fill of its `av_malloc` block made every image sweep run away (formats wave-1 review, >1500 s / 14 GB → 71 s with one `ptr::write_bytes`); a byte-by-byte silence check made `ub_safe/samplefmt.rs::every_owner...` 460 s → 1 s (compare with slice `==`). Thousands of sub-slices over one block (4096 planar channels) still run away even with slice ops: thin the count under `CRUSTIFY_FFMPEG_VARIANT=bsan`.
- Runaway: freeing a C (`av_calloc`) block after a Rust `&[u8]` view of >=32 KiB grows RSS without bound; for C strings (`HeapString::as_bytes`, `av_asprintf`/`av_append_path_component` results) even 4-8 KiB runs away. ~1 KiB stays at seconds and <1.2 GB. Avoid 2 GiB successful allocations.
- Whole-image/whole-buffer sweeps run away under BSan: at formats wave 1, `imgutils::images` tests over every pixel format at real sizes and `samplefmt::buffers` tests with many planar channels each ran >25 min alone (up to 62 GB RSS) and stalled the gate's equiv and ub_safe binaries past 2 h, while plain/ASan/TSan took seconds. Root cause was the per-byte retags above; after fixing those, every-format image sweeps are still minutes each, so under `CRUSTIFY_FFMPEG_VARIANT=bsan` they keep one format per layout class (`sweep_formats()` in `equiv/imgutils.rs` and `ub_safe/imgutils.rs`), two sizes and two alignments, rather than skipping; time each new heavy test alone under BSan before landing (`<test binary> --exact <name>`).
- Moving a `Box` or taking `&mut self` on a Rust-built C struct whose fields point into the same allocation retags it and invalidates the inner raw pointers (Miri too): keep it behind `Box::into_raw` and project with `&raw mut (*p).field`.
- False positive: a variadic C call with many stack-passed pointer arguments (`av_sscanf` with 16 destinations) gets dead-frame provenance for some and reports "allocation that has been freed" at C's write; register-passed ones (first four variadic args) are fine. Skip such calls under `CRUSTIFY_FFMPEG_VARIANT=bsan` with a comment.
- This runtime no longer reports the old `realloc(NULL, n)` / `av_realloc` false use-after-free (the opt skip was removed in the core wave-0 batch-2 review): treat remaining `bsan` skips citing it as stale and re-test them.
- False positive, sequence-dependent: BSan reports "access an allocation that has been freed" when C reads through an *uninitialised local* that uninstrumented glibc `strtol`/`strtoull`/`strtod` wrote (`av_channel_layout_from_string` `channel_layout.c:342` `*endptr`, `:417` `*end`). The store updates the bytes but not the slot's shadow, which keeps the provenance an earlier frame left there: a previous `av_channel_layout_from_string` call that `av_freep`'d its `av_strdup` list (`3c` then `ambisonic 1` fails in a pure-C probe; `ambisonic 1` alone or after `stereo` passes). Which inputs trip it depends on the caller's frames, so under `CRUSTIFY_FFMPEG_VARIANT=bsan` skip every description that reaches those lines: all `ambisonic ...` forms and everything `parse_channel_list` rejects (masks, `3c`, `4C`, `5 channels`, misspellings); standard names and accepted channel lists (`FL@a+FR`, `2 channels (FL+FR)`) are safe. `av_channel_from_string`'s `USR<n>` initialises its `endptr` to the string first, so it is fine. Shared predicate: `tests/common/channel_layout.rs::parsed_before_strtol` / `allowed(&DESCRIPTIONS)` (formats wave-2 batch-0 review, `misc/bsan-stale-stack-shadow-under-glibc-strtol-end-pointers`, with the C probe).
- False positive, multi-threaded only: a test that installs a Rust `log::set_callback::<C>()` process-wide while another suite logs (formats wave-0 batch-1 review: `equiv/imgutils.rs` + `cpu::parse_caps`) makes BSan report "access an allocation that has been freed" at `av_strtod`'s `next[0]` on the *other* thread, though `next` (written by uninstrumented glibc `strtod`) points inside the live `av_expr_parse` block; it comes and goes with unrelated callback edits or a gdb stop, and `--test-threads=1` passes. Run such callback-installing integration tests alone in a child (`current_exe() --exact <name> --test-threads=1` + env marker, as `equiv/imgutils.rs::isolated` and `ub_safe/log.rs::isolated` do since that review) rather than skipping them under bsan (triage: `crustify/reviews/20261005/libavutil/formats/wave-0/batch-1/misc/bsan-stale-provenance-in-av-strtod-under-rust-log-callback/`); `equiv/log.rs` and `ub_unsafe/log.rs` still install callbacks unisolated and could flake the same way.
- False positive, stack-layout dependent: every `av_expr_parse` primary goes through `av_strtod`, whose glibc-written `next` keeps a stale slot shadow (often a previous parse's freed `w` buffer), so BSan reports a use after free at `eval.c:120` (`next[0]`), or at `eval.c:429` (`*next == '('`, a primary that is no number; options wave-1 batch 0). Reproduced from pure C: two parses, the second after `alloca(pad)`, trip at pads 0/240/512/1200 of 0..2048. Options wave-0 batch 1 makes its `Expr::parse` tests return early under bsan, and batch 4's exhaustive `eval::parse_number` sweeps (89k strings, `ub_safe/eval.rs::short_strings`, which trip it single-threaded, ASan clean) do too; listed-input tests pass; anything else that parses through the evaluator (`av_opt_set` of numbers, `av_expr_parse_and_eval`) can flake the same way, string-typed option elements do not. Build such a C probe with `$TC/bin/clang -g -fpass-plugin=$TC/lib/libbsan_plugin.so -I/target -I$B probe.c -rdynamic -Wl,--whole-archive $TC/lib/libclang_rt.bsan-x86_64.a -Wl,--no-whole-archive $TC/lib/libbsan_rt.a -L$B/libavutil -lavutil -Wl,-rpath,$B/libavutil -lgcc_s -lm -lpthread -ldl` (`TC=/root/.rustup/toolchains/bsan`, `B=/target/crustify/builds/bsan`) and run with `BSAN_SYMBOLIZER=$TC/bin/llvm-symbolizer`.
- `cargo bsan run` of a standalone reproducer fails with `libavutil.so.61: cannot open shared object file` (exit 127): the bsan libraries are not on the binary's rpath. Run the built binary, which lands under `$CARGO_TARGET_DIR/bsan/x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu/debug/` (an extra `bsan/` level), with `LD_LIBRARY_PATH=$B/libavutil:...` (`B=crustify/builds/bsan`) and `BSAN_SYMBOLIZER=/root/.rustup/toolchains/bsan/bin/llvm-symbolizer` (options wave-0 batch-1 review).
## Miri
- Miri cannot call FFmpeg: run only FFI-free tests by name filter. Filters are substrings (`intreadwrite::alias64_` also catches FFI tests): add `--skip heap` or list exact names.
- `Rational`'s `PartialEq` calls C's `av_cmp_q`, so every `==` on `Rational`/`CieXy`/`PrimaryCoefficients`/`ColorPrimariesDescriptor` is FFI: run csp/rational unit tests under Miri with `--skip equality` (formats wave-3 batch 0).
- Miri randomizes the address of align-1 locals, so a `[u8; 8]` local is usually not 8-aligned: alignment-sensitive tests need a `#[repr(C, align(8))] struct Aligned([u8; N])` buffer.
## Coverage
- C, without touching the shared build: run tests with `CRUSTIFY_FFMPEG_VARIANT=coverage GCOV_PREFIX=<private dir> GCOV_PREFIX_STRIP=0`; the `.gcda` lands under `<dir>/target/crustify/builds/coverage/libavutil/`. Copy the `.gcno` files there, `ln -s /target src` (objects record `src/libavutil/<f>.c`), and run `gcov -b -c -f -o <that dir> src/libavutil/<f>.c` from it. The build is gcc 14 and only gcov 13 is installed: it warns about the version but reads the data; `llvm-cov gcov` rejects the `.gcno`.
- Rust: `RUSTFLAGS="-C instrument-coverage" LLVM_PROFILE_FILE=<dir>/%p-%m.profraw cargo +nightly test`, then the nightly rustlib's `bin/llvm-{profdata,cov}`. Test binaries live under `<target>/debug/build/ffmpeg/<hash>/out/<name>-<hash>`, not `debug/deps`; `llvm-cov report` takes the first binary positional and the rest as `-object`.
- A workset of C enums (and by-value POD structs with no lifecycle routine) has no C path of its own to gcov: measure Rust only and say so in the README. `llvm-cov show` marks a line executed even when one `cfg!`-selected arm never ran (`native_endian`'s big-endian arm shows as an "Unexecuted instantiation" note, not a zero-count line); that is the only miss left in formats wave-0's four type homes (review 2026-10-10).
- A review with no code defect still measures both cheaply: one gcov-variant crate run (~3 min) plus `gcov` over the touched `.c`, and one instrumented run with `llvm-cov report` over the home `.rs` files.
## Tests and conventions
- `av_opt_set` of a COLOR that does not parse copies `set_string_color`'s uninitialised `uint8_t tmp[4]` into the field, exactly like IMAGE_SIZE's `tmp[2]`: equivalence tests must mask both after a failing set (plain builds happen to agree; TSan's stack layout does not). `av_opt_show2` lists only options whose flags intersect `req_flags`, so the fixture's flagless options never reach C's default/range printers; `equiv/opt.rs::objects::listings_of_every_type_and_extreme_log_as_c` lists a flagged copy of the table (options wave-0 batch-1 review).
- Wrappers that *preview* a value with C's evaluator before C evaluates it again (`opt.rs` `number_value`) must see the value C will: refuse `time()` (it changes between the two evaluations; a sanitized debug build needs `T` up to ~500 us ahead of the clock to straddle) and give an array option's `default` C's `DEFAULT_NUMVAL`, the definition's address bits (options wave-0 batch-1 review).
- Wave-0 enum newtypes carry a private `const fn name(self)` for `Debug`, which collides (E0592, across modules) with a public `name()` wrapping C's `*_name` getter. Formats wave-1 batch 3 renamed it to `constant_name` on the `pixfmt.rs` types and `Channel`; `SampleFormat`, `MatrixEncoding`, `ChannelOrder` and `MediaType`-style types still have `name`. Static C name getters there return `Option<&'static CStr>` (round-trips with `from_name(&CStr)`).
- `clippy::not_unsafe_ptr_arg_deref` (deny by default) fires on a safe setter that only *stores* a raw-pointer argument into a field (`addr_of_mut!((*self.as_mut_ptr()).opaque).write(p)`): a local `#[allow]` with a one-line reason (formats wave-1 batch 1, `ChannelCustomMut::set_opaque`).
- Fixed `char name[N]` fields C fills with `av_strlcpy` keep stale bytes after the NUL (`parse_channel_list` reuses one `AVChannelCustom` for every parsed channel): equiv tests compare such fields up to the NUL, not byte for byte, unless both sides were written by the same strlcpy-shaped sequence.
- bindgen backs a C enum with no negative enumerator by `c_uint` (`AVMatrixEncoding`), so a `MediaType`-style `index()` with `raw >= 0` warns `unused_comparisons`; check the generated `pub struct X(pub c_uint|c_int)` first.
- New trybuild cases: run the `compile_fail` test once, move only your `.stderr` files from `crustify/rust/ffmpeg/wip/` into `tests/ub_safe/compile_fail/`, then delete `wip/` (it holds a `.gitignore`, so `git status` never shows it).
- An E0599 `.stderr` whose help is "there is a method `x` with a similar name, but with different arguments" quotes the library's source line, so it breaks when that line changes; pick a missing method whose suggestion only rewrites the test's own line (`white_point_mut` on a `Ref` suggests `white_point()` in place).
- trybuild normalizes line numbers, but an E0277 `.stderr` against a sealed or crate trait lists its implementors, so it breaks when a later wave adds one: use a `compile_fail,E0277` doctest there. Private-field E0423 `.stderr` is mostly stable; a toolchain update can still add a diagnostic note, so re-bless with `TRYBUILD=overwrite` only after checking the diff is purely diagnostic.
- Tests that install the process-wide log callback (`log::set_callback`) must act only on their own thread's messages (a thread-local flag) and hold the module's shared `LogState` lock: sibling suites log from many threads.
- A `ClassRef` (AVClass) for any raw test class reaches integration tests through the safe API via `tests/common/class.rs::with_class(obj, |class| ...)`. In the `equiv` binary, reuse `crate::log::class_fixture::with_class` (made `pub(crate)` by options wave-0 batch 2) rather than including `class.rs` again (`clippy::duplicate_mod`); hold `crate::log::LogState` while calling it. In `ub_safe`, `cpu::parse_cpu_caps(&mut f, c"no-such-flag")` logs in C's `cpuflags` class context and also `Eval`'s.
- Handle seams (`FooRef::as_ptr`, `from_ptr`) are `pub(crate)`: integration tests cannot compare a returned `PixelFormatDescriptorRef<'static>` with C's pointer. Identify a C table entry by its contents plus its `name` pointer (each named entry has its own literal), as `equiv/pixdesc.rs::descriptor_table::ident` does, and pass C's own pointer (`av_pix_fmt_desc_get`) to raw reference calls.
- `#[path = ...] mod x;` inside an inline `mod` resolves from a directory that does not exist, and including `tests/common/opt.rs` twice in one binary trips `clippy::duplicate_mod`: reuse the existing `pub(crate) mod fixture` include (opt.rs, tests/equiv/opt.rs, tests/ub_unsafe/opt.rs).
- `Foo::zeroed()` is ffibox's `CZeroable` trait method, which the `ffmpeg` crate does not re-export: give a value type an inherent `new()` (and `Default`) that calls it.
- Type-punning unions with no C routine (av_alias*, av_intfloat*) and function-like macros (`AV_IS_INPUT_DEVICE`) get a C-executed reference through `crustify_<NAME>` shims in `libavutil-sys/shim.{h,c}` (include the defining headers; `crustify_.*` is already allowlisted). The shim compiles with every variant and passes ASan/TSan/BSan. Macros are not wavefront nodes and never get scheduled: a reviewer adds them and files a `misc` report. Constant macros bindgen cannot evaluate (a shift of an enum constant like `AV_CH_FRONT_LEFT`, compound literals like `AV_CHANNEL_LAYOUT_STEREO`; `var AV_CH_.*` in the allowlist yields nothing) become Rust constants composed as the header composes them, with a `crustify_<FAMILY>(size_t i, const char **name[, T *out])` table shim as the C reference (`crustify_AV_PIX_FMT_NE`, `crustify_AV_CH`, `crustify_AV_CH_LAYOUT`, `crustify_AV_CHANNEL_LAYOUT`); check `crustify/wavefront/deprecated.json` `ff_api_gated_macros` to leave the deprecated aliases out. Digit-leading C names get a letter prefix (`XRGB` for `0RGB`, `masks::L5POINT1` for `AV_CH_LAYOUT_5POINT1`).
- A wrapper that re-implements C control flow around per-token C calls must mirror C's exact numeric path (truncation vs `llrint` rounding). A differential probe (a standalone package under `/tmp/<batch>/probe` depending on `ffmpeg` with `default-features = false, features = ["libavutil"]` and its own `[workspace]` table) ran 300k random inputs against `av_parse_cpu_caps` in seconds and found a divergence the hand-written suite missed.
- Nudging a csp coordinate past `av_csp_primaries_id_from_desc`'s 1/1000 tolerance with `den / 50` is a no-op for SMPTE 428's equal-energy white point (`{1, 3}`): use `num * 1000 + den * 2` over `den * 1000` (formats wave-3 batch 0).
- `av_csp_primaries_id_from_desc` reports BT.709 for any description with a `0/0` coordinate or two zero-denominator ones (the all-zero `Default` included): the summed delta becomes `0/0`, `av_cmp_q` returns `INT_MIN` and C reads `< 0` as a match. `ColorPrimariesDescriptorRef::identify` returns `None` for any zero denominator (formats wave-4 batch 0); equiv tests that call the raw C on Rust storage (`rust_id`) still see BT.709 there. The function is UB-free for every `int` input (3M-input UBSan fuzz).
- Any C comparison built on `av_add_q`/`av_sub_q` (e.g. `av_csp_primaries_id_from_desc`'s `< 1/1000`) is exact only while the results' parts fit an `int`: `av_reduce(..., INT_MAX)` rounds a coordinate whose denominator exceeds `INT_MAX / 100000` to the closest `int` rational (about `2e-13` near 1/1000; `311418082/999095547` as BT.709's `wp.x` is `1e-13` inside the tolerance yet reduces to exactly 1/1000 and identifies as nothing). Do not document such a wrapper as exact arithmetic; pin it with an `i128` one-coordinate exact model vs C (`equiv/csp.rs::reduced_sums_follow_c_not_exact_arithmetic`; a ctypes `Fraction` probe found the fixtures in seconds, formats wave-4 review).
- `csp.c`'s `color_primaries` table holds identical entries for SMPTE 170M and SMPTE 240M, so `av_csp_primaries_id_from_desc` returns 170M (6) for 240M's own description (first entry within tolerance): compare a rebuilt gamut against C's identification of the reference (`c_id_at(&reference)`), never against the table index (formats wave-3 batch-0 review). `AVDOVIDmLevel9/10` (bound, `Default`) are the C structs that embed an `AVColorPrimariesDesc` by value, usable as a `#[repr(C)]` mirror parent in tests.
- Log-capturing equiv tests outside `equiv/log.rs` take its `LogState` lock (`pub(crate)` since formats wave-0 batch 7, `use crate::log::LogState`) and mask ` @ 0x...` in formatted lines: C's own contexts (`IMGUTILS` etc.) live on C's stack and move between calls.
- C code paths that SIMD replaces on this host (`block_sad_*_c`, `av_image_copy_plane_uc_from`'s plain path) are covered by a lib unit test that re-runs itself alone in a child (`current_exe() --exact <name> --test-threads=1` plus an env marker) and calls `av_force_cpu_flags(0)` / a detected subset there; works under ASan/TSan/coverage.
- Hand-written asm can need more than the C contract says: check `libavutil/x86/*.asm` for `mova`/`movntdqa`/legacy-SSE memory operands (16-byte alignment) and rounded-up widths before trusting a header's "undefined if ..." list (formats wave-0 batch 7: `av_image_copy_plane_uc_from` segfaults on misaligned rows).
- An exactly ending heap buffer at a chosen alignment offset under `#![forbid(unsafe_code)]`: allocate `vec![0; pad + len].into_boxed_slice()` and retry with the corrected `pad` until `(ptr + pad) % 64` matches (tests/ub_safe/imgutils.rs `placed`).
- ffibox's `CVec` docs say raw-pointer elements are `Send + Sync` by default, but its impls require `T: Send`/`T: Sync`: a `HeapVec<*mut u8>` (a C plane-pointer array) is `!Send`/`!Sync`, so an owner holding one needs its own proven `unsafe impl` (formats wave-1 batch 5, `SampleBufferWithPointers`).
- `CVal<Foo, P>` (a by-value owner, e.g. `ChannelLayoutVal`) cannot get `Clone` (orphan rule: `CVal` is foreign), so a C deep copy goes on the shared handle as `try_clone()`. Equivalence tests that pass a Rust-held `CVal` to C readers cast `ptr::from_ref(&val).cast::<ffi::Foo>()` (`CVal` is `#[repr(C)]`, value first, ZST policy; const-assert the sizes), since the handles' `as_ptr` is `pub(crate)`.
- `ChannelCustom::from_raw` / `ChannelCustomMut::set` can put a 16-byte name with no NUL into a layout's custom map; C's `av_channel_layout_describe(_bprint)` (`%s`) and `index_from_string`/`channel_from_string` (`strcmp`) then read past the field. Wave-3 wrappers of those readers must check each map name for a NUL first (formats wave-2 batch 0 documented it on `ChannelLayout`).
- `AVChannelCustom` is `{int id; char name[16]; <4 padding bytes>; void *opaque}` (32 bytes): an unterminated `name` read as a C string stops at the (calloc-zeroed) padding, so ASan cannot catch C's `%s`/`strcmp` over-read of it even at the block end; the wave-3 batch-1 readers' NUL guard (`names_terminated`) is checked by its verdict, not a sanitizer. `av_channel_layout_ambisonic_order` squares `floor(sqrt(nb - popcount - 1)) + 1` in `int`: ambisonic counts >= 46340^2 + 1 overflow (unreachable via `set_ambisonic`, guarded anyway).
- C range checks of a `strtol`-fed `int` can themselves overflow: `av_channel_layout_from_string`'s `order + 1 > INT_MAX / (order + 1)` is UB for an order that narrows to exactly `INT_MAX` (`2147483647`, `0x7fffffff`, `-2147483649`, `6442450943`; UBSan `channel_layout.c:341`), after which C accepts a 0-channel ambisonic layout. Grep a wrapped parser for `strtol`/`strtoll` results used in `int` arithmetic and probe the `INT_MAX`/`INT_MIN` spellings (whitespace, sign, hex/octal, modulo-2^32 narrowing) with a C harness. `channel_layout.rs::c_strtol` is a glibc-exact `strtol(s, &end, 0)` mirror (checked against libc on 20k random strings) to reuse for such guards (formats wave-3 batch-1 review).
- C's `FFALIGN(x, a)` is `x + a - 1` in `int`: a range check of `x <= INT_MAX - (a - 1)` still overflows at the boundary (`av_samples_get_buffer_size`, align 0, `nb_samples == INT_MAX - 31`; UBSan `samplefmt.c:137`). Grep a wrapped routine's `FFALIGN`s for this before trusting its own guards.
- C UB the opt.h setters reach through `av_set_options_string`/`av_opt_set` with a well-formed class: a `color` value with a NaN opacity (`red@nan`, `parseutils.c:433`); a failing `image_size` value `memcpy`s `set_string_image_size`'s uninitialised `tmp[2]` into the field (unspecified, not UB: compare such fields only after successes). `parseutils::parse_color*` refuse NaN opacities and prefixes ending inside `#`/`0x` (`slen == hex_offset - 1` reads an uninitialised buffer; `slen = INT_MAX` overflows); `set_options_string` keeps both in its `# Safety` (options wave-0 batch 4).
- `opt.h` typed getters (options wave-1 batch 0): `av_opt_get_video_rate` forwards to `av_opt_get_q`, whose `read_number` has no `VIDEO_RATE` case, so C fails with `EINVAL` on every video rate option; `ObjectRef::get_video_rate` reads the field (documented divergence). `av_opt_get_array` needs a zeroed output (the copy path frees what is there, the fail path frees every element), reads a binary element's length past it (refused), and converts float/double/rational elements to `int64_t` unchecked (previewed as `f64`, `ERANGE`). `tests/common/opt.rs::ArrOwned`/`ARR_CLASS` hold one array of each element type (binary empty, no default) and a `child_class_iterate`; C's `get_format` log swaps its arguments ("option 'pixel' is not a int format").
- `opt::ObjectRef`/`ObjectMut` (options wave-0 batch 3) are the borrowed handles over an `AVOptions`-enabled object (unsafe `from_ptr` seam; `BareObject` is the safe class-only object for `ub_safe` and log contexts). Further C UB on well-formed classes the wrappers refuse: eval builtins converting doubles unchecked (`ld`/`st`/`random`/`randomi`/`taylor` index, `print` level, `gcd`/`bitand`/`bitor`; `random` after `st`/`root`; shared guard `eval::refuses`), so any numeric `av_opt_set` string can be UB (`"ld(1e300)"`); `+`/`-` flag tokens outside int64 (`+1e30`, a `CONST` of `INT64_MAX`); rationals whose bounds exceed `int` (`(int)num` in `write_number`); `av_opt_set_int/double` on an array option (int stored over the array pointer, invalid free at `av_opt_free`); `av_opt_set_bin/image_size` on an array option (`opt_type_desc[o->type]` read out of bounds in the error log); `av_opt_get_int` of float/double/rational values outside int64; any setter with `AV_OPT_SEARCH_FAKE_OBJ` on a non-runtime option (NULL deref). `av_opt_free` leaves binary lengths stale (later `av_opt_get`/`av_opt_copy`/`is_set_to_default` read the NULL buffer): C references in equivalence tests must reset them. Use `tests/common/opt.rs::OBJ_CLASS` / `Owned` as the well-formed object fixture: the older `CLASS` has a type-99 option outside the object contract. Under BSan, `av_opt_set`/`set_defaults` paths hit the strtod/strtoul end-pointer false positive (`eval.c:120`, `parseutils.c:391`): the object suites return early there and BSan covers typed setters on zeroed objects (`ub_unsafe/opt.rs::typed_paths_across_reborrows`).
- Arrays of `AV_OPT_TYPE_BINARY` overrun in C: `opt_type_desc` sizes an element as one pointer, but `set_string_binary` / `opt_copy_elem` write (and `opt_get_elem` reads) the `int` length after it (ASan heap-buffer-overflow `opt.c:373`). Options wave-1 batch 1 refuses non-empty values for them in `value_refusal` (so `set`, `set_from_string`, `set_options_string`, `set_from_dict`) and every `insert_array`/`replace_array`; batch 0's `get_array` refuses them too. Equiv/UB fixtures keep a `bins` array empty.
- `av_opt_set_chlayout` (and `av_channel_layout_copy` generally) on ENOMEM leaves the destination `CUSTOM` with a NULL map and `nb_channels > 0`, which `av_opt_get` then describes through NULL: `ObjectMut::set_channel_layout` resets it to the zeroed layout (`reset_unmapped_layout`). `av_timegm` does its date and time-of-day sums in unchecked `int` (UBSan `parseutils.c:577/585/587` for |year| beyond ~5.88M or hours beyond ~596k): `parseutils::timegm` mirrors them with checked ops and returns `None`.
- `av_opt_set_array`'s removal path (NULL `val`) frees and memmoves before its shrinking `av_realloc_array`; under an `av_max_alloc` limit below the new size it returns ENOMEM with the old count kept (double free at `av_opt_free`). `ObjectMut::remove_array` writes the new count back after that ENOMEM (options wave-1 review, `ub_unsafe/remove-array-shrink-failure-double-free`). Equiv tests that drive C under allocation limits can silently compare such corrupted C state: repair the C reference as the wrapper does. Also, C converts a RATIONAL value into an integer-like array element as `llrint(1/den)*num` (`3/2` -> 0), unlike `av_opt_set_q`'s rounded quotient.
- `opt.rs` has two sealed array traits since options wave 1: `ArrayElement` (batch 0, owned outputs of `get_array`: `Option<HeapString>`, `Dict`, `ChannelLayoutVal`) and `ArrayInput` (batch 1, borrowed inputs of `insert_array`/`replace_array`: `&CStr`, `Option<&CStr>`, `ChannelLayoutRef`, `Option<DictionaryRef>`, plus the plain types). `tests/common/opt.rs` has two array fixtures: batch 0's `ArrObj`/`ArrOwned` (defaults for every type) and batch 1's `Arrays`/`OwnedArrays` (child, size bounds, wide rationals, read-only, scalar; `zeroed_with_child()` skips C's default parses, which BSan misreports at `eval.c:120`, so BSan-targeted tests use it).
- `av_expr_parse`: parenthesis/call nesting past 100 is `EINVAL` (`stack_index`), but a tree deeper than 100 levels (a chain of >100 binary operators, or a call around a 100-deep chain) is reported as `AVERROR(ENOMEM)`. `av_expr_eval` has the float-to-int builtin UB listed above (UBSan `eval.c:206/240/258/264/276/338/352`; shared guard `eval::refuses`) and reads `const_values[const_index]` unchecked: `AVExpr` keeps no constant count, so a safe eval wrapper must carry the count `Expr::parse` was given (options wave-0 batch 1 left `parse` returning a plain `ExprBox`; options wave-1 batch 0 added `eval::Expression`, the evaluable owner keeping the count and refusing `refuses(s, 0)` at parse: `av_expr_eval`'s parser logs `print()` with offset 0).
## scan-unsafe
- `undocumented_unsafe` wants the `// SAFETY:` line right above the line holding `unsafe {`: when rustfmt wraps `let x =\n    unsafe { .. }` the comment above `let` counts as undocumented though clippy accepts it. Shorten the expression (bind a local first) so `let x = unsafe { .. };` stays on one line, and re-check after `rustfmt` (formats wave-1 batch 4).
- `undocumented_unsafe` looks back at most 6 lines from the `unsafe {` line for `SAFETY`: a longer `// SAFETY:` block counts as undocumented although clippy accepts it. Move the general part into a plain comment above it and keep the `// SAFETY:` block at 6 lines or fewer (formats wave-3 batch 2).
- A private helper taking `&self` on a `define_ctype!` layout type (`fn fields(&self)` on `Foo`) counts as `ref_to_type_wrapper_smell`; trait impls (`PartialEq::eq(&self, ..)`) are sanctioned. Put such helpers on `FooRef` and call `self.as_ref().helper()` (formats wave-0 batch 4).
- To diff counters against the base commit, scan a `git worktree add --detach /tmp/<batch>/base HEAD` copy, but first `ln -s /target/crustify/builds <copy>/crustify/builds`: `crustify/builds` is an untracked symlink, and without it the `-sys` build scripts fail and the scan prints "no counts". `undocumented_unsafe` has per-file totals only, no sites. Remove the copy with `git worktree remove --force` afterwards.
## Wavefront
- Symbol `ptr` blocks for an `AVFoo **` slot: `scalar: {"by_ref": {"owned": true, "borrowed": null}}` (booleans, not nested blocks), argument lifetimes as `"arg:<name>"`; a dropper's slot needs top-level `owned: true` (as `av_opt_freep_ranges`), and a routine that allocates into the slot is `is_constructor: {alloc: true, init: false}` (alloc and init are exclusive; options wave-0 batch 0, `av_dict_free`/`av_dict_copy`/`av_dict_set`).
- Array parameters (`uint8_t *data[4]`, `int linesize[4]`) are missing from `ptr_args`, so `av_image_alloc`'s ownership of `pointers[0]` cannot be recorded (`lifetime.for` must name a recorded pointer arg; `--update` rejects it). Submit `{"ptr_args": {}, "lifetime": null}` and keep the finding in the code docs (formats wave-1 batch 4).
- Pass the worktree, not `/target`, as `REPO_ROOT`: `state_dir` is relative, so `wavefront /target ... --update` writes the main checkout's `ownership-store.json` instead of the one your batch commits.
- `query types|symbols --update` takes a PATH to the findings JSON (inline JSON fails with "File name too long"). Keep each submission in a file outside the worktree so it reapplies verbatim after a rebase; a review keeps it as its report's `submission.json`.
- Types take `_comment_agent`; symbols reject it, so fold reasoning into each `ptr.note`. A field-less type (C enum) is marked submitted with `{"fields": {}, "_comment_agent": ...}`, a pointer-less symbol with `{"ptr_args": {}, "lifetime": null}`. `ptr_args` keys are C parameter positions (a `fmt` after an `int` is `"2"`), and a `const` innermost pointee forces `mutable: false` even for a written `T const **` slot.
- Each symbol update takes ~20-60 s and each enum footprint query ~2 min: run batches of them in the background with a long timeout.
- `query types --users` / `--lifecycle-ops` return `[]` for C enums (footprints cover struct/union field access only): grep the public headers for an enum's users. `query symbols --taking/--returning <Type> --api-only` is a quick check of a type's pointer-based lifecycle (`AVRational` has none).
## Landing in a parallel wave
- Expect 2-3 non-fast-forward rejections per landing in a busy wave (core wave 0: 12+ sibling commits in ~3 h; batch 13 rebased six times). Siblings share header homes, `tests/*/main.rs`, `libavutil-sys/{allowlist.txt,shim.c,shim.h}` and `crustify/wavefront/ownership-store.json`. Read the wave's other worklists in `crustify/campaigns/<id>/schedule.json` up front: siblings can wrap related items (a static inline and the function it forwards to) into the same home under the same name, or add a same-named compile_fail file.
- `tests/*/main.rs`, `allowlist.txt`, `shim.{c,h}`: plain union (keep both `mod` lines sorted, both blocks, the landed side's includes); `shim.*` blocks share a closing `}`, so compile the `-sys` crate after resolving.
- `ownership-store.json`: on a textual conflict, take the wave side (`git checkout --ours` during the rebase) and re-run your `--update` submissions; never hand-merge. A clean auto-merge can be kept after a 3-way check (key `symbols` by `(name, defined_in, variant)`, each entry equal to the side that changed it, none changed by both, `types` untouched).
- When re-taking `ownership-store.json` during a rebase, copy it from the commit being rebased onto (`git show :2:<path>`, or the onto SHA; a clean auto-merge has no stage 2, so use `git show $(cat "$(git rev-parse --git-path rebase-merge/onto)"):<path>`), not from the live wave branch name: a sibling can land mid-resolution, and the branch tip then carries their entries into your commit as unrelated adds (formats wave-0 batch 1 hit this; it showed as deletions against the new tip).
- Shared home `.rs` files: keep your additions as self-contained, delimited sections (trailing `mod <name> { use ...; }` blocks in test files, test items prefixed with the type name), so a rebase resolves by taking the landed side (`git show :2:<path>`) and re-appending your own: wave doc + union of `use`s + wave items + your items + merged `unit_tests`. Keep the landed copy of a duplicated private helper, and match conventions a sibling already landed in the module.
- A rebase can merge cleanly yet break when a sibling calls a private macro or helper whose signature you changed: grep its call sites and `cargo check` after every rebase.
- Re-validate the whole crate after every rebase before pushing, under every instrument (not only the suites you touched: a sibling's change can break your dependents and yours theirs), and push promptly, since the wave moves while long passes run.
- Review siblings file overlapping tool reports: after a rejection, grep the landed `crustify/reviews/.../wave-<n>/*/` and trim a duplicate to a cross-reference. A review batch that finds no defect lands nothing, or only a test commit.
## Review reproducers
- A reproducer package under `crustify/reviews/<campaign>/<lu>/<sub>/<wave>/<batch>/<kind>/<slug>/` reaches `crustify/rust/ffmpeg` through eight `..` (`../../../../../../../../rust/ffmpeg`, as `core/wave-0/batch-12/ub_safe/memcpy-backptr-block-length-overflow/Cargo.toml` has it), seven from a package directly under `<batch>/` (a kept differential probe); count the components from `crustify/` down (formats wave-3 batch-1 review corrected an earlier "nine" here). Seed its `Cargo.lock` from `crustify/rust/Cargo.lock` so `--offline` resolves; test an ffibox fix with `--config 'paths=["<ffibox worktree>"]'`.
- A reusable differential probe for `pixdesc::read_image_line`/`write_image_line` (a literal transliteration of C's loops with every `int` op checked for UB, deciding accept/refuse for random descriptors, planes and lines) is kept at `crustify/reviews/20261005/libavutil/formats/wave-2/batch-1/probe/` (`cargo run -- <rounds> <seed>`; 300k rounds in ~3 s plain, ASan-buildable); a 40k-round copy is `tests/equiv/pixdesc.rs::random_lines` (500 rounds under bsan, ~20 s). The model-vs-wrapper shape (independent C model + exactly sized 0xFF planes) suits any wrapper whose safety rests on mirrored C arithmetic.
- A reusable C-vs-Rust random-sequence probe for `AVBPrint` (every appender, init shape, truncation, `av_max_alloc` toggles, both finalizers) is kept at `crustify/reviews/20261005/libavutil/core/wave-2/batch-0/probe/` on the wave-2 review branch; copy its `main.rs` shape for other stateful wrappers.
- The `-sys` crates' rpath link args do not reach a dependent binary ("libavutil.so.61: cannot open shared object file"). Either add a direct `libavutil-sys = { path = ... }` dependency and a `build.rs` printing `cargo:rustc-link-arg=-Wl,--disable-new-dtags` and `-Wl,-rpath,$DEP_AVUTIL_LIB_DIR` (as `ffmpeg/build.rs` does), or run it with the variant's `lib*` dirs on `LD_LIBRARY_PATH`.
- A sanitized reproducer needs `CRUSTIFY_FFMPEG_VARIANT=asan-ubsan RUSTFLAGS=-Zsanitizer=address cargo +nightly build -Zbuild-std --target x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu`, or linking fails on `__asan_*`. UBSan recovers by default, so have `main` re-exec itself with `UBSAN_OPTIONS=halt_on_error=1` and exit with the child's status.
- To capture the trace on the affected commit, swap the home `.rs` with `git show HEAD:<path>` while the fix is uncommitted, build, run, restore.
